ex with Hsp70</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-285750" lvl="1" marL="742950" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="480"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="–"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none" cap="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>when hormone binds, Hsp70 dissociates</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-228600" lvl="2" marL="1143000" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="400"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2000"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2000" u="none" cap="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>receptor dimerizes</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-228600" lvl="2" marL="1143000" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="400"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2000"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2000" u="none" cap="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>exposes nuclear localization region</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-285750" lvl="1" marL="742950" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="480"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="–"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none" cap="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>so hormone-receptor complex travels to nucleus to be transcriptional activator</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-190500" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="480"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:t></a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr b="0" i="0" sz="2400" u="none" cap="none" strike="noStrike"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:cxnSp><p:nvCxnSpPr><p:cNvPr id="695" name="Google Shape;695;p96"/><p:cNvCxnSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvCxnSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="309562" y="990600"/><a:ext cx="8458200" cy="0"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="straightConnector1"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln cap="flat" cmpd="thinThick" w="57150"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="2FB0DC"/></a:solidFill><a:prstDash val="solid"/><a:miter lim="800000"/><a:headEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/><a:tailEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:cxnSp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="Two diagrams, a and b, illustrate the mechanisms of steroid hormone receptor function. In part a. there’s a cross-section of a curved yellow background, labeled as cytoplasm, on top of a blue curved section, labeled as nucleus. At the top of the yellow section is a curved, darker yellow border, sandwiched by two lines; this border is labeled as plasma membrane. Above the plasma membrane is a small orange pill, labeled Hormone. Below that is an arrow that points down to a blue structure with a slightly pointed left corner. To the right, it is attached to a green rectangular structure with a rounded bottom; the two pieces fit like a puzzle – this structure is labeled NR-Hsp70 complex. Below that is an arrow pointing down, with another arrow jutting right to the green rectangular structure described previously; this is labeled Hsp70. The arrow pointing down to the same blue structure, but this time, it is bonded to the hormone at the top left; this is labeled NR-hormone complex. Below it, another arrow points down to two NR-hormone complexes, sitting back-to-back; this is labeled NR dimer. The NR dimer sits on top of a curved blue line, which serves as a boundary between the cytoplasm and the nucleus. An arrow points from the dimer down to the dimer sitting on top of a blue rectangular line labeled DNA on the left. The top right of the dimer is connected to a green ovular structure, labeled Coactivator. To the bottom right of the dimer is a tan, folded structure, labeled Pol II. To the right of this on the blue line, there is a yellow segment on the line, labeled Target gene, and to the right, the rest of the line is blue. Between the blue section after Pol II and the Target gene is an arrow that points slightly up and right to green text that reads, “ON.” From ON, an arrow curves up to a wavy line labeled mRNA. Another short arrow points up to another mRNA structure, followed by another arrow that points up to a blue cloud, labeled Protein. From here, another arrow points up to “Change in cell function.” The first mRNA is in the nucleus, but the remainder of the arrows and structures point up into the cytoplasm. Going back to the nucleus, where the dimer sits on the DNA is shaded deeply and labeled HRE. Below HRE, an arrow points down. To the left of the arrow are two green lines that form a loop at the left end; at their right end is a connected vertical line; this is labeled lncRNA. An arrow from this structure curves into the arrow pointing down to the NR dimer; however, this time, the dimer is slightly slanted left, with the lncRNA lodged in the middle. Below the structure is the same DNA structure as described earlier. This time the arrow between the blue and yellow segments points to red text that reads, “OFF.” " id="696" name="Google Shape;696;p96"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="5237162" y="1143000"/><a:ext cx="3735387" cy="5553075"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>